WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.560
SPEAKER: It's
important to protect

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:05.130
your API from unauthorized
access, and one way to do this

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:06.780
is with API keys.

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:08.880
And so in this
episode, we will cover

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:13.510
how to create an API proxy
that requires an API key,

00:00:13.510 --> 00:00:16.170
and then also how to
create an API product,

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:19.740
how to add a developer,
how to register an app

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:22.690
and then call your
API with an API key.

00:00:22.690 --> 00:00:26.610
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:29.190
When an app makes a request
to an API proxy that

00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:31.560
is configured to
verify an API key,

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:33.750
the app must supply a valid key.

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:36.690
At runtime, the
Verify API Key policy

00:00:36.690 --> 00:00:38.970
checks that the
supplied API key is,

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:43.260
one, valid, hasn't been
revoked, and matches the API

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:46.110
key for the API
product that exposes

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:48.270
the requested resources.

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:51.180
If the key is valid,
the request is allowed.

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:53.280
If the key is
invalid, the request

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:55.840
results in an
authorization failure.

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:58.560
So let's first start
off by creating a proxy.

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:00.960
Visit apigee.google.com.

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:05.640
Next, click Develop,
API Proxies, Create New.

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:07.620
This is the Build
a Proxy wizard.

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:11.880
Select Reverse Proxy, which
is the most common, FYI.

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:14.760
Enter the following
values like this.

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:15.930
Click Next.

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:19.470
On the Common Policies
page, select API Key.

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:23.580
This option automatically adds
two policies to your API proxy.

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:24.750
Click Next.

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:27.900
On the Summary page, select the
Optional Deployment checkbox

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:30.270
for your environment.

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:32.850
Next, click Create and Deploy.

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:35.370
And finally, select
Edit Proxy to view

00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:38.190
the overview of the API proxy.

00:01:38.190 --> 00:01:41.220
From here, you can view the
deployment status, which takes

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:44.100
a few seconds to complete.

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:46.380
To view the policies that
were added by the wizard,

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:48.270
let's select the Develop tab.

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:49.740
You'll see that
two policies have

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:54.540
been added to the proxy request
proof flow of the API proxy.

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:57.480
One is Verify API
Key, which checks

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:01.260
the API call to make sure
a valid API is present,

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:03.100
sent as a parameter.

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:05.640
The second is a
Remove Query Param API

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:08.610
Key, which is an assigned
message policy that

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:11.820
removes the API key after it's
checked so that it doesn't

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:13.830
get exposed unnecessarily.

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:17.370
Click the Verify API Key
Policy icon in the flow view

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:21.180
and look at the policy's XML
configuration in the lower code

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:22.260
view.

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:24.720
The API Key element
tells the policy

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:27.660
where it should look for the
API key when the call is made.

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:30.810
By default, it looks for the
key as a query parameter called

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:34.080
APIKey in the HTTP request.

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:36.390
The name APIKey is not special.

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:40.410
It can be any property
that contains the API key.

00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:42.660
Next, let's try to call the API.

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:45.030
First, lets directly
access the target.

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:48.030
In a web browser, go to
the following address--

00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:50.670
mocktarget.apigee.net.

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:53.850
This is a target service
the API proxy is configured

00:02:53.850 --> 00:02:55.740
to forward requests to.

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:59.890
You should get this successful
response, "hello guest."

00:02:59.890 --> 00:03:01.890
Now let's make a
call to the API proxy

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:04.980
to see how it's being
protected by the policies.

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:06.780
We will make a
curl command using

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:08.910
the external
internet-facing host name.

00:03:08.910 --> 00:03:11.820
If you do not have one set up,
I have included instructions

00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:13.980
in this video's description.

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:16.350
Note that in my
case, the external IP

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:18.360
provided by the
external load balancer

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:22.080
is configured with a
DNS registered hostname.

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:26.130
We can call the API using curl
and a command line terminal,

00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:29.190
such as the Cloud Shell from
your Google Cloud project

00:03:29.190 --> 00:03:31.530
at console.google.com.

00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:33.780
Because of the Verify
API Key policy,

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:35.700
you get the following
error response

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:38.430
since it did not
pass a valid API key.

00:03:38.430 --> 00:03:40.080
If we didn't
configure that policy,

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:42.030
you would get the same
successful response

00:03:42.030 --> 00:03:43.540
we saw before.

00:03:43.540 --> 00:03:46.440
Now close your Cloud terminal.

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:49.650
[? great, ?] so now
let's get an API key.

00:03:49.650 --> 00:03:51.600
First, we'll create
an API product

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:55.320
by returning to the Apigee
console and select Publish, API

00:03:55.320 --> 00:03:56.700
Products.

00:03:56.700 --> 00:04:00.090
And click on the +API Product.

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:03.030
Enter the product details
for your API product.

00:04:03.030 --> 00:04:04.680
First, an internal name--

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:07.260
and note you cannot
use special characters.

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:10.170
And once created, you
cannot change it later.

00:04:10.170 --> 00:04:12.180
Next, enter the
display name, which

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:13.950
can use special characters.

00:04:13.950 --> 00:04:17.730
Or you can let it default to the
name which is used in the UI,

00:04:17.730 --> 00:04:19.870
and you can edit it at any time.

00:04:19.870 --> 00:04:22.770
Next, add a description
of your API product.

00:04:22.770 --> 00:04:25.080
Then check the box to
have it allow access

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:27.840
to your eval environment,
and change the access

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:29.250
level to Public.

00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:32.160
Also check the box to
Automatically Approve Access

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:33.120
Requests.

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:36.750
You can leave Quota and the
Allowed OAuth Scopes unfilled

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:37.900
for now.

00:04:37.900 --> 00:04:41.460
Next, scroll down a bit to the
API resources section and click

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:45.250
Plus, then select the API
proxy you just created,

00:04:45.250 --> 00:04:46.500
then click Add.

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:52.032
In the Path section, click Add
a Path and enter a single slash.

00:04:52.032 --> 00:04:53.940
Click Save.

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:57.090
Next we're going to simulate the
workflow of a developer signing

00:04:57.090 --> 00:04:59.040
up to use your APIs.

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:02.220
A developer will have one or
more apps that call your APIs,

00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:05.040
and each app gets
a unique API key.

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:08.130
This gives you, the API
provider, more granular control

00:05:08.130 --> 00:05:11.220
over access to your APIs
and more granular reporting

00:05:11.220 --> 00:05:13.380
on API traffic by app.

00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:16.080
So let's create a developer
by selecting Publish,

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:19.410
Developers in the left
menu, and click +Developer,

00:05:19.410 --> 00:05:21.870
and enter the following in
the New Developer window,

00:05:21.870 --> 00:05:23.340
and click Create.

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:26.680
Now let's register an app
by selecting Publish, Apps,

00:05:26.680 --> 00:05:28.330
and click +App.

00:05:28.330 --> 00:05:30.940
Enter the following data in
the New Developer App window.

00:05:34.180 --> 00:05:35.830
Click the Developer
box to select

00:05:35.830 --> 00:05:39.800
a developer you just created.

00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:42.010
Then in the Credential
section, ensure Never

00:05:42.010 --> 00:05:44.800
is selected so that the
credentials for this app

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:46.960
never expire.

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:48.857
Next, click Add
product and select

00:05:48.857 --> 00:05:50.065
the product you just created.

00:05:52.830 --> 00:05:55.750
And finally, click Create.

00:05:55.750 --> 00:05:59.470
Now let's get the API key by
clicking Show in the Key field.

00:05:59.470 --> 00:06:03.310
You can access this page later
by going to Publish, Apps,

00:06:03.310 --> 00:06:05.290
and selecting your app.

00:06:05.290 --> 00:06:07.420
Notice that the key is
associated with the product

00:06:07.420 --> 00:06:09.190
you created.

00:06:09.190 --> 00:06:11.320
Select and copy
the key, which you

00:06:11.320 --> 00:06:14.710
will paste as a query parameter
using the documentation's

00:06:14.710 --> 00:06:18.820
template, and ensure
there are no extra spaces.

00:06:18.820 --> 00:06:21.190
Next, open your Cloud
terminal once again

00:06:21.190 --> 00:06:23.120
and paste this curl document.

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:25.280
And now when you
call the API proxy,

00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:28.630
you will get "Hello,
Guest" as a response.

00:06:28.630 --> 00:06:29.590
Congratulations.

00:06:29.590 --> 00:06:32.140
You've created an API
proxy and protected it

00:06:32.140 --> 00:06:36.400
by requiring that a valid API
key be included in the call.

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:38.140
However, note it's
a good practice

00:06:38.140 --> 00:06:40.900
to pass the API key
in a header rather

00:06:40.900 --> 00:06:42.580
than in a query parameter.

00:06:42.580 --> 00:06:44.380
The reason is because
headers do not

00:06:44.380 --> 00:06:46.780
appear in the browser
history or network

00:06:46.780 --> 00:06:50.500
logs, which could present
a possible security risk.

00:06:50.500 --> 00:06:54.320
And so I have linked
instructions on how to do so.

00:06:54.320 --> 00:06:56.410
And there you have it--
a quick walk-through

00:06:56.410 --> 00:07:00.190
on how to protect your API
from unauthorized access.

00:07:00.190 --> 00:07:02.230
And friends, if you found
this episode helpful,

00:07:02.230 --> 00:07:04.580
you can click Like or
subscribe to the channel.

00:07:04.580 --> 00:07:05.080
Cheers.

00:07:05.080 --> 00:07:10.230
[MUSIC PLAYING]

